is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router7
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: OSPF adjacency on the Campus interconnect link. The interface router7.eth3 (10.0.0.1/24) that forms the OSPF peering to router10.eth2 (10.0.0.2/24) is down.

Mechanism: With router7’s eth3 down, the OSPF neighbor to router10 on 10.0.0.0/24 dropped. OSPF reconverged, removing the directly connected path and installing a longer alternate route via router8 and router17. The added hops explain the higher but not catastrophic latency users observe.

Localization: On router7, “ip addr” shows eth3 in state DOWN despite having 10.0.0.1/24 configured, and “show ip ospf neighbor” has no neighbor on eth3 (only neighbors on eth2, eth4, and eth5). Router7’s routing table now learns 10.0.0.0/24 via OSPF through 172.25.1.2 (eth5) instead of as a connected network, confirming loss of the local link. Router10’s eth2 remains UP, indicating the failure is on the router7 side of the link.

Root cause: Physical or administrative down state on router7.eth3 caused the OSPF adjacency to router10 to fail.

Corrective fix: Restore the WAN link link_wan_10_0_0 between router7.eth3 and router10.eth2. Specifically, bring router7.eth3 back UP (check for admin shutdown, cable/port issues), verify OSPF is enabled on eth3, and confirm the OSPF neighbor to router10 reestablishes. This will restore the direct, lower-latency path between Campus 1 and Campus 2. 
=== END ===